WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.572
A pivot table creates a cross-tab summary that is easy to compare.

00:00:04.572 --> 00:00:10.897
In this example, region becomes the rows and channel becomes the columns.

00:00:10.897 --> 00:00:18.462
The amount column provides the values, and we sum those values for each region and channel combination.

00:00:18.462 --> 00:00:22.344
The result looks more like a report than a raw DataFrame.

00:00:22.964 --> 00:00:29.041
Pivot tables are useful when people need to scan across categories quickly.

00:00:29.041 --> 00:00:36.271
For example, a manager may want to compare online, store, and email sales across several regions.

00:00:36.271 --> 00:00:43.591
A regular groupby can calculate the same information, but the pivot table may be easier to read.

00:00:43.591 --> 00:00:46.706
Notice the fill value option in the code.

00:00:46.706 --> 00:00:53.866
If a region does not have a particular channel, Pandas can place a zero instead of leaving the cell empty.

00:00:53.866 --> 00:01:00.406
Run the example, then try changing the aggregation function from sum to mean.

00:01:00.406 --> 00:01:05.350
The table layout stays the same, but the meaning of the values changes.
